// =============================================================================
//  server.hml — THE AUTHORITATIVE HOST.                              W4-11.
// =============================================================================
//
//  v1 is single-player. This file is still a SERVER, and that is the whole
//  point (NETWORKING §5): a client and a server in one process, talking over
//  an in-memory transport, so that the day a socket appears nothing above it
//  has to change. Nothing in here knows a window exists.
//
//      server_new(seed, transport)         build the world, run the boot script
//      server_tick(sv, tick)               ONE fixed tick: drain -> step -> publish
//      server_apply_command(sv, ps, cmd)   the direct path (tools, tests)
//      server_snapshot(sv)                 THE PACKET. Bytes. What a client reads.
//      server_local(sv)                    THE OWNER BLOCK. hp/ammo/xp/beats/fx.
//
//  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  THE TWO THINGS THAT LEAVE THIS FILE, AND WHY THERE ARE EXACTLY TWO
//  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  1. `server_snapshot(sv)` is a `src/sim/snapshot.hml` snapshot: a real 13-byte
//     header and one quantised record per entity, written by the real writer.
//     `client.hml` decodes it with the real reader. That round trip is not
//     ceremony — it is the only reason the v2 client is a decode call instead of
//     a rewrite, and it is what keeps the client structurally unable to read
//     `world.*`. (BUILD_PLAN W4-11: "the renderer never reads world.*".)
//
//  2. `server_local(sv)` is the OWNER BLOCK — the per-player state that is not
//     in the entity stream because only one client may ever see it: hp, the
//     magazine, xp, the lantern channel, the wave state, and this tick's
//     cosmetic queue. NETWORKING calls it the local block and reserves it in
//     the protocol; here it is a struct, and in v2 it is `S_SNAPSHOT`'s owner
//     section. One struct, filled in one place, read in one place.
//
//  There is deliberately no third channel. If the client needs something new it
//  goes in one of those two, which is what stops "just read the world for this
//  one thing" from happening the first time it is convenient.
//
//  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  THE SCRIPT — GAME_DESIGN §8, "the first 60 seconds", is IN THIS FILE
//  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The sequence is authoritative, so it is server-side, so it is deterministic
//  from the seed and the tick and nothing else. Every beat below is either a
//  literal from GAME_DESIGN §8 or a constant from `core/config.hml`; the times
//  are TICK offsets from the session start, because a wall clock cannot be
//  replayed. `tools/probe_game.hml` runs the whole minute headless and asserts
//  the beats land, which is only possible because no beat is frame-driven.
//
//      0:00  black, a bell, "The lamps went out on a Tuesday."   (client card)
//      0:03  the camera stands up (0.8 s) and control is yours    (client)
//      0:06  the unlit lantern is 40 m ahead, downhill            SPAWNED AT t0
//      0:10  three Wisps drift up over the crest                  tick t0+600
//      0:18  a Mote rises from behind a rock                      tick t0+1080
//      0:24  you reach the post: HOLD [E] - LIGHT THE LANTERN     (client prompt)
//      0:27  the 2.5 s channel                                    sim stage (g)
//      0:30  LIGHT. +350 XP, LEVEL 2, the unlock card             sim + client
//      0:33  the dusk horn, and four Husks come up the hill       HERE, on the lit edge
//      0:36  wave 1, tier 1                                       director_begin
//      0:56  WAVE 1 CLEARED, 0.4 s of silence, ember rain         director event
//      0:58  a path of lit lanterns, downhill and to the left     HERE, on the clear edge
//
//  "It's going to get their attention" is not flavour text: the horn and the
//  hold are triggered by the LANTERN, not by the clock. `director.hml`'s own
//  header says `director_begin` is called by the lantern channel completing;
//  W5-6 will own that call, and until it exists the integrator owns it. If the
//  player never lights it, the day cycle's own horn edge still arms the
//  director and night still comes. The world does not wait.
//
//  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHAT THIS FILE MAY NOT DO
//  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  No SDL, no wobbleweed, no renderer, no `__clock()`. It is not walled by
//  `ci_imports.sh` (src/game may import everything) — it is walled by taste,
//  and by the fact that `tools/probe_game.hml` links it headless and asserts
//  `nm | grep -c SDL_ == 0`. The clock lives in `main.hml`; the arithmetic that
//  turns milliseconds into ticks lives in `sim.hml`'s accumulator; this file
//  only ever sees an integer tick number.
// =============================================================================

